{"question_id": "caf03d32", "hypothesis": "Here are the most common slow cooker mistakes and how to fix them, tailored to what you're cooking:\n\n**Flavor depth**\n- **Sear before you slow-cook.** Even with vegetables, a quick 5-minute sear in a hot pan before adding them to the pot builds caramelisation that the slow cooker alone can't. This is the single biggest upgrade for stews and curries.\n- **Use broth instead of water.** Vegetable broth (or a good mushroom/soy-based broth for vegan) adds layers that plain water never will.\n\n**Texture problems**\n- **Cut vegetables evenly.** Carrots and potatoes cook slower than zucchini or peppers. Group by cook time: hard root veg on the bottom, soft veg on top.\n- **Don't overcook delicate items.** If a recipe calls for fresh herbs or citrus, add them in the last 30 minutes.\n- **Beans and lentils:** If using dried, soak overnight first. Canned beans go in for the last hour to avoid going mushy.\n\n**Liquid ratio**\n- Slow cookers trap moisture, so use **about 25% less liquid** than a stovetop recipe. If your stew is soupy, that's the likely culprit. You can always thin it with more broth, but you can't un-thin it.\n\n**Lid and timing**\n- **Don't lift the lid frequently.** Every time you open it, you lose 15\u201320 minutes of effective cooking time.\n- **If it's running dry**, add a splash of broth rather than water.\n\n**Vegan/vegetarian-specific tips**\n- **Umami boosters:** A spoonful of miso, a splash of soy sauce, tomato paste, or nutritional yeast go a long way in replacing the depth that meat broth provides.\n- **Fat matters:** A tablespoon of olive oil or coconut oil added at the start helps carry flavour.\n- **Your cashew yogurt experiment:** Add it near the end (last 15\u201320 min) so it doesn't break or turn grainy.\n\nSince you've been making beef stew and are exploring vegan options, a good next recipe to try: **slow cooker tomato and white bean stew** \u2014 sear onions and garlic, add canned white beans, tomatoes, broth, and a spoonful of miso. Cook on low for 6\u20137 hours. It's forgiving and shows off all of these principles.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
